    <briefdescription>
<para>A globally hashed type represents a hash value that is sufficient to uniquely identify a record across multiple type streams or type sequences. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This works by, for any given record A which references B, replacing the <ref refid="classllvm_1_1codeview_1_1_type_index" kindref="compound">TypeIndex</ref> that refers to B with a previously-computed global hash for B. As this is a recursive algorithm (e.g. the global hash of B also depends on the global hashes of the types that B refers to), a global hash can uniquely identify that A occurs in another stream that has a completely different graph structure. Although the hash itself is slower to compute, probing is much faster with a globally hashed type, because the hash itself is considered &quot;as good as&quot; the original type. Since type records can be quite large, this makes the equality comparison of the hash much faster than equality comparison of a full record. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
